I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the supplied tungstate workflow/tooling first, then identify candidate phases and carry a quantitative GSAS‑II refinement through to exported CIFs and profiles. I’ll keep the phase count evidence-driven and flag any ambiguity in the final report.

The chemistry narrows the plausible inventory to Mn–W–O products plus residual Mn₂O₃/WO₃, but at 500 °C partial reaction and multiple polymorphs are credible. I’m now extracting the peak inventory and checking the frozen COD/DARA and GSAS‑II interfaces so candidate selection is based on the full profile rather than a few diagnostic lines.

Search–match isolates two crystalline components: monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the dominant 23–25° triplet and 33–35° group, while bixbyite-type Mn₂O₃ explains the independent residual sequence near 23.4, 33.3, 38.5, 45.4, 49.6, 55.4, and 66.0°. MnWO₄ and Mn₃WO₆ leave their strongest expected peaks unexplained and are not supported at a meaningful level. I’m moving to full-pattern GSAS‑II refinement to resolve the close WO₃/Mn₂O₃ reference variants and quantify the fractions.

The first two-phase Rietveld model is converging now. I’m refining calibration, both cells, phase-specific broadening, and the per-phase isotropic displacement factors in stages; after that I’ll compare the close structural variants on the same parameterization, rather than choosing the database entry from search–match alone.

The variant matrix is running in parallel. The initial unconstrained fit exposed the expected profile correlations, so I bounded the instrument terms to the measurement model and separated common instrumental width from phase-specific size/strain in alternating stages. That is yielding a more defensible comparison and prevents a candidate from “winning” by absorbing structural errors into nonphysical peak widths.

The refined residual is not random: its strongest positive sequence (34.48, 34.14, 50.22, 48.53, 47.53°) coincides with additional low-symmetry WO₃ reflections. I’m therefore reopening the phase count and testing triclinic/orthorhombic WO₃ models and a possible WO₃ polymorph mixture. This is stronger evidence than forcing the two-phase model through texture or excessive broadening.

The alternative single-phase WO₃ tests are still refining; the P2₁/m model already failed decisively. In parallel, the residual match to triclinic WO₃ remains a live hypothesis, but I will only add it if its refined scale stays positive and it removes unique reflections across the pattern—not merely the crowded 23–25° region.

A third WO₃ phase is not yet justified: a ~4.6 wt% duplicate monoclinic component improves the fit, but a two-parameter uniaxial microstrain model along WO₃ [100] achieves essentially the same reduction with far fewer parameters and matches an allowed specimen effect. I’m retaining the two-phase interpretation while testing texture and the Mn₂O₃ profile; the extra phase remains a fallback only if unique peaks survive.

I’ve narrowed the pattern to a two-phase WO₃/Mn₂O₃ model. I’m now resolving whether the WO₃ distortion is genuinely triclinic or adequately monoclinic, then I’ll consolidate the physically constrained refinement and export the requested deliverables.

The triclinic branch gains almost nothing from preferred orientation, whereas the monoclinic branch requires a much stronger texture correction. That is useful evidence for a genuinely lower-symmetry WO₃ phase, but I’m checking it against model complexity and the generator’s physical bounds before committing.

I’m rebuilding the monoclinic model from the published parent coordinates and enforcing the generator’s physical ranges. This avoids letting the lower-symmetry alternative win merely by moving weakly scattering oxygen sites far beyond the allowed reference mismatch. I’ll compare fixed specimen-height branches because height, zero, and lattice parameters are strongly correlated.

The physically constrained monoclinic refinement is stable at about 6.4% Rwp before the small coordinate corrections. The remaining discrepancy is concentrated in peak intensities and widths, not unexplained reflections; the phase inventory remains two phases.

The profile scan found one important point: the apparent 8,000 ppm strain boundary was not driving the solution. Polarization has a modest effect, and surface roughness is at most marginal. I’m now validating candidate projects by fresh GSAS-II replay, because cached post-refinement curves can otherwise make very small Rwp differences misleading.

The constrained solution has converged to approximately 75.1 wt% monoclinic WO₃ and 24.9 wt% orthorhombic Mn₂O₃. I’m regularizing the weak oxygen coordinates to the known reference-mismatch scale; that costs only ~0.02 percentage points in Rwp but avoids reporting spuriously precise, over-displaced oxygen sites.

The final GPX and CIFs pass the structural and replay checks. One submission-format check caught that the angle-column label was too verbose for the scorer’s alias set; I’m correcting that header and rerunning the end-to-end validation.

A residual check warrants one last audit: a second WO₃ polymorph previously absorbed about 5 wt% when the peak-shape model was less mature. I’m retesting that candidate against the final constrained profile to distinguish a real minor phase from a broadening surrogate.

The minor-polymorph scale falls to only ~1.6–2.6 wt% against the mature model. Its apparent benefit is small and highly correlated with cell/profile terms; I’m waiting for its physical size/strain refinement before deciding whether it is identifiable.

The audit confirms the second WO₃ polymorph is not independently identifiable: it only models broad residual tails at the crystallite-size lower bound. I’ve retained the defensible two-phase result and documented that ~2 wt% ambiguity explicitly. Final consistency checks are now complete.

Completed. The refined crystalline composition is:

- WO₃, monoclinic P2₁/c: 75.10 wt%
- Mn₂O₃, orthorhombic Pbca: 24.90 wt%

Fresh GSAS-II replay gives Rwp 5.874%, Rp 4.269%, and GOF 2.209. A possible ~2 wt% secondary WO₃ polymorph was not reliably distinguishable from peak-shape mismatch and is documented as uncertainty.

